nd the problem but our goal is  always to make the user able to use  their system again fast and then figure  it out after the fact with post-mortems  what happened and make sure that it  doesn't happen again exactly and  wherever possible to build system  to do that and build it into the code  rather than relying on humans to come in  and fix something very cool and so when  we were talking off-camera you were  mentioning some really interesting  design patterns that that sre deals with  could you talk about that sure so one of  the challenges in hosting a massive  deployment of kubernetes clusters which  I talked a little bit about earlier is  that the traditional SME model for a  hyper scalable system is to build a  multi-tenant system and what that means  is that all of your query flow and user  action is flowing through a single  binary or a single replicated set of  binaries now a more traditional monolith  for a single user is what we call a  single tenant system so for example an  Oracle database it has one user and it's  completely specified for that user and  it's been tweaked and perfected for  exactly their use case but it's really  only useful to one person at once the  challenge in hosting these kubernetes  clusters is that every single kubernetes  cluster is in its own way from my point  of view a single tenant system you know  this system belongs to one user this  system belongs to a different customer  they don't have any interaction with  each other and as an operator I can't  really treat them as a monolith so we're  trying to develop some new operations  patterns for how we deal with that and  keep them all running when they're so  different from one another and that's  what we've been calling starting to call  multi single tenant systems got it is  this a pattern that customers could  potentially apply in their own systems  absolutely whenever you have a customer  who is building something that has many  separate users pipeline or shop  deployment or anything along those lines  there are very similar patterns to keep  in mind it definitely helps with  scalability of their solutions for sure  I think one of the key principles that  we're starting to come up with is really  that automation is king and that  monitoring and trying to make sure that  you can have some equivalent monitoring  between each of your single tenant  systems because it doesn't matter  whether you are using your kubernetes  cluster to host a machine learning  pipeline and I'm using mine to host  a YouTube front page both of us have a  control plane that has uptime and it's  meaningful to measure control plane  uptime and I can compare our two  clusters with that that's super  interesting let's take a step back and  talk about a little bit about your  background and how you got into sorry so  I joined Google in 2012 as a new  graduate with a master's in CS degree  and I went straight into the Google Ads  SME team okay sorry not at all I had  offers from three separate teams and one  of them was an SME and I went to some of  my friends at Google I said what's an  SME and they said well they wear leather  jackets and they swear a lot and they're  always really on the go and stressed out  I'm like that sounds great  sign me up oh man and it seems like  you've enjoyed it since yeah what do you  recommend for new grads who are trying  to follow a similar journey what should  they look into and what are the kind of  things that can help them determine  whether to follow this Ari path or a  different path  well one of the things that they might  want to have a look at is the SOE book  we recently came out with the SME  workbook and I actually have a write-up  of one of the big gke  outages in that workbook if people are  curious and another thing is just try  spinning up some systems and try  breaking them don't just try to make  them work try to break them and figure  out how they break and if that's  interesting to you then this might be an  area to pursue yeah I think there might  be a lot of folks out there that are  interested in that so thank you for that  let's switch gears a little bit and talk  about coupon this is your first coupon  order your impressions it's huge  yes it is it's really really big I was a  little surprised about the gender  diversity here my essary team is about  half women which is a little bit unusual  but I was not expecting it to be quite  as masculine what can we do to improve  that I mean I think that it's really a  question of looking at how the community  treats various people I know that in the  past I've had some challenges with a  very feminine name in open-source  getting code reviews it feels like you  get a bit more pushback but also just  one of the things that  I did notice that coupe con is working  on is having technical women on stage  right all of the keynotes have had a  huge number of really impressive women  like Janet and Liz and that's something  I super appreciate not as awesome and  finally let's wrap up with what are you  looking forward to most to keep on is  there a talk or whatever honestly I am  looking forward to talking to other  people who are using kubernetes in ways  that we are not right I feel like that's  what I get the most out of is one-to-one  conversations figuring out where  people's pain points are how they're  different from mine because I have a bit  of a weird perspective on kubernetes I  don't use coupon a tease I run  kubernetes right so it's a bit of it and  if we can figure out how customers are  using it in ways that we don't expect we  can make it more robust and reliable  over time that's the idea that is the  role of SRE thank you very much me thank  you pleasure talking to you  [Music]
 

